**Read:** [Analytical Chemistry 2.1 — David Harvey](../sources/Analytical-Chemistry-Harvey.lingua#r_0XMY75E3FRXRGR3BK28V3JAC0T), chapter 6’s polyprotic/distribution treatment; [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), polyprotic and log-concentration/pH lessons; [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapter 14’s polyprotic examples.

A polyprotic component can occupy several protonation states linked by successive equilibria. The analytical total conserves the component across those species. Charge balance includes their different charges and all other relevant ions. Well-separated constants may permit regional approximations, while overlapping steps need a coupled treatment.

For an ideal diprotic concentration model with h = [H⁺], constants K₁,c and K₂,c, and D = h² + K₁,c h + K₁,cK₂,c, the fractions are α₀ = h²/D, α₁ = K₁,c h/D, and α₂ = K₁,cK₂,c/D. They sum to one. Multiplying by the analytical total gives each species concentration under this model.

These functions express distribution at a specified h; they do not independently determine the pH of a closed system. That requires the other balances and constraints. Activity corrections, complexation, precipitation, or an open reservoir change the complete model and can change which apparent species fraction is relevant to a measurement.

**Work:** derive the fraction formulas from equilibrium and mass balance, verify normalization and limits, and plot them over an identified range. Compare well-separated and overlapping hypothetical constants. Solve a charge-balance problem using the fractions rather than choosing one dominant form without checking.

Read the complete polyprotic and distribution-diagram treatment. The same mathematical structure reappears in ligand binding, coordination, and biological protonation, but each application must identify its own species and valid constants. Preserve the shared derivation and specialize only the unique chemistry.

